course: dictionary
lesson: 4133
title: Words that shine
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word glitter can be a noun or a verb. A noun names a thing: «covered in glitter» means covered in tiny shiny pieces. A verb is an action: «The stars glitter» means the stars shine with many small flashes of light. Look at the words around it to see which one it is.
explain: Context clues help you find the meaning. In «That is how they catch fool's gold, pyrite, which glitters like gold but is harder», the word like tells you glitters means shines the way gold shines. Ask: what is the sentence about? Then try your meaning in place of the word and check it still makes sense.
explain: Glitter does not always mean something good. «All that glitters is not gold» is a saying: things that look shiny and special may not be special at all. So when you read glitter, think about what is shining and whether the shine tells the whole story.
fact: Glitter can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action).
fact: As a verb, «glitter» means to shine with many small flashes of light, like jewels or stars.
fact: As a noun, «glitter» means tiny shiny pieces of coloured plastic or metal used to decorate cards and crafts.
fact: Glitter has no plural, so we say «some glitter», not «glitters» for the noun.
fact: Things glitter because they reflect light from many small surfaces, like the dew drops on a sundew's leaves.
exercise: «The glitter on the card was everywhere, even on the table.» What is glitter in this sentence? | *Tiny shiny pieces used for decoration | A small green insect | A kind of wet weather | A loud noise
exercise: «The lake began to glitter as the sun came out.» What does glitter mean here? | To freeze solid | To sink slowly to the bottom | To make a loud sound | *To shine with many small flashes of light
exercise: «Pyrite glitters like gold but is much lighter.» Why is pyrite called fool's gold? | Because it is real gold, just cheaper | Because only fools can find it | Because it is made of plastic | *Because it looks like gold but is not real gold
test: «Glitter is kept away from eyes, because it is hard to clean and hurts eyes.» Why must glitter be kept away from eyes? | It makes the room too hot | *It is hard to clean and it hurts eyes | It is too heavy to lift | It melts in the sun
test: Jayden reads «All that glitters is not gold». What does the saying mean? | Glitter should be eaten | *Things that look good may not be good | Only shiny things are valuable | Gold is always found in lakes
exam: «On an autumn morning a garden fence can glitter with dew drops.» What kind of word is glitter here? | A noun, a thing: the fence is made of glitter | A noun meaning a spider | *A verb, an action: the fence shines | A verb meaning to sink
